congress is taking a major step to  combat hate crimes against  asian americans the senate  overwhelmingly passed a bill to expand  the federal government's role  this follows a recent surge in violence  related to the covet 19 pandemic  particularly against the asian american  community  congressional correspondent nicole  killian joins us now from capitol hill  nicole  it's pretty rare to see lawmakers come  together like this  what would this legislation actually do  absolutely michelle this bill was a  bipartisan effort and the lawmakers who  spearheaded this  hope it sent a powerful message not only  to the asian-american pacific islander  community  but to all americans the measure cleared  the senate  94-1 the lone no vote was missouri  republican josh hawley who felt it was  too broad  the coven 19 hate crimes act would  designate a point person at the  department of justice  to expedite the review of covet-19  related hate crimes it also provides  more guidance to state and local law  enforcement agencies to make it easier  to report hate crime incidents  and it expands public awareness  campaigns the legislation comes  just over one month after several women  of asian descent were killed in a mass  shooting in atlanta  from the start of the pandemic about 3  800  anti-asian crimes have been reported  president biden applauded the senate  action in a tweet writing acts of hate  against asian americans are wrong  un-american and must stop the bill now  heads to the house  for a final vote next month vlad  nicole thanks very much
 

